<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Hydaspes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/326-bce">326 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-alexander-the-great">Wars of Alexander the Great</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Hydaspes was fought in 326 BCE on the banks of the Hydaspes (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and the Indian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>. Alexander crossed the swollen river upstream of Porus' army under cover of a storm and defeated it despite its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. Impressed by Porus, he restored him as ruler, but his exhausted troops soon refused to march further east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes.jpg" alt="Battle of the Hydaspes" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Fontebasso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_the_Great_(356-23_BC)_and_Porus_(oil_on_canvas).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> defeated the last Achaemenid forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> and Spitamenes in 328 BCE, he began a campaign towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 327 BCE. He entered India through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, and a smaller force under his own command took the fortress of Aornos. In the early spring of the next year he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, against his neighbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>, who had refused to surrender and was preparing for war.</p><p>Alexander had to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> to keep marching east, since leaving a strong opponent on his flank would have been dangerous, and he could not show weakness to the Indian princes already subdued. Porus chose the ground well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Alexander camped on the north bank of the Hydaspes, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, and in the spring of 326 BCE Porus drew up on the south bank. The river was too deep and fast for a direct crossing, so Alexander moved his cavalry up and down the bank each night with Porus shadowing him. He found a crossing about 27 km upstream, where a wooded island divided the river, and left Craterus in camp with most of the army to make feints. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> he took 6,000 foot and 5,000 horse. A storm that night drowned the noise of the crossing. Porus's son, sent to meet them, was routed and died.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> came with the best of his army, with cavalry on the flanks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> in front and elephants before the infantry. Alexander attacked the flanks with cavalry first, and the Indian horsemen were routed. The elephants then advanced against the Macedonian phalanx and caused heavy losses, but were driven back and trampled their own side. The phalanx advanced while the cavalry charged from the rear, and Craterus, who had crossed, pursued the fleeing army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>, who had fought on, finally dismounted and was taken to Alexander. Asked how he wished to be treated, he is said to have replied, treat me as a king would treat another king. Alexander let him keep his lands and made him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>. He founded two cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nikaia</a> at the site of the battle and Boukephala across the river in honour of his horse Bucephalus, which died during or after the battle.</p><p>Alexander then marched east to the Hyphasis, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>, where his troops, who had campaigned nearly eight years, refused to go on. He turned south along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The battle exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> to Greek political and cultural influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Hydaspes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the <b>Hydaspes</b> (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in siege warfare alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of the Hydaspes</b>, the Jhelum, in 326 BCE, Porus faced Alexander with his elephants; Alexander crossed the river by surprise and won.</blockquote><a href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
